WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.024
The day after opening matters as much as the opening
celebration.

2
00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:10.762
Cedar Crossing identifies who checks the room, how
problems are reported, and who follows up with
facilities staff.

3
00:00:10.962 --> 00:00:16.638
Shared service expectations work better than repeated
informal requests with no clear owner.

4
00:00:16.838 --> 00:00:21.484
During any disruption, users need to know what remains
available and where to go.

5
00:00:21.684 --> 00:00:23.681
Staff need current information too.

6
00:00:23.881 --> 00:00:31.637
Emergency preparation also requires a plan, defined
responsibilities, communication arrangements, training,
and review.

7
00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:33.782
Include evening and weekend staff.

8
00:00:33.982 --> 00:00:41.378
For actual emergencies, follow your institution's
current procedures; this lesson is not an
emergency-response guide.

9
00:00:41.578 --> 00:00:45.117
After people use the space, ask whether it works as
intended.

10
00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:52.454
Gather staff and user feedback about access, comfort,
function, and safety concerns, and respond to what you
learn.

11
00:00:52.654 --> 00:00:58.659
Post-occupancy evaluation can reveal an affordable
adjustment or improve the next project.

12
00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.439
Planning does not end when the furniture arrives.

